.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt FORK 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fork ,
.Nm vfork ,
.Nm clone ,
.Nm clone3
.Nd create a child process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft pid_t
.Fn fork "void"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn vfork "void"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_clone" "unsigned long flags" "void *stack" "pid_t *parent_tid" "void *tls" "pid_t *child_tid"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_clone3" "struct clone_args *args" "size_t size"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn fork
creates a child process with its own process record, address space, and
file descriptor table.
User pages are copied on write: parent and child initially share their
contents, but a write by either process does not change the other's memory.
.Pp
The child inherits the parent's program name, credentials, supplementary
groups, working directory, root directory
.Pq Xr chroot 2 ,
file creation mask, resource limits, session,
process group, nice value, dumpable flag and no_new_privs bit, and not its
parent-death signal; see
.Xr prctl 2 .
Its CPU times start at zero.
It inherits signal dispositions and the blocked signal mask;
it inherits no pending signals.
If signal work is pending when the call begins, the kernel takes it first
and restarts the call, even when the handler has no
.Dv SA_RESTART .
A signal sent to several processes during the fork is also kept for the
child, as on Linux; this includes terminal process-group signals.
A child whose caller's process begins to end while it is being made
runnable is sent
.Dv SIGKILL .
The inherited program name remains visible in
.Pa /proc/<pid>/stat
until
.Xr execve 2
replaces it.
.Pp
Inherited descriptors refer to the same open file descriptions as the
parent's descriptors.
They share file offsets and status flags.
Parent and child can close their descriptors independently.
.Pp
The soft
.Dv RLIMIT_NPROC
limit bounds the number of threads with the parent's real user ID, the
parent's included: each thread of a process counts, as on Linux, and one
that has ended counts until it is reaped.
A child being built reserves its count before any building step can sleep.
It bounds a new thread as it bounds a new process.
A parent with real user ID 0 is exempt.
.Pp
.Fn vfork
creates a child that shares the parent's address space instead of a copy
of it, and the parent does not return from the call until the child calls
.Xr execve 2
or ends.
Every store the child makes, on the stack included, is the parent's, so
the child should do nothing else before one of the two.
An
.Xr execve 2
that fails returns to the child, and the parent still waits.
A signal to the parent does not end its wait unless it is fatal to the
process:
.Dv SIGKILL ,
or a signal whose action is the default and ends the process without a
core dump, such as
.Dv SIGTERM ,
under the rule Linux applies as the signal is sent.
A thread must take it at once: the thread it was sent to, or for a signal
sent to the process another of its threads when that one does not, takes
it at once when it does not block the signal, has not begun to end, is not
stopped, and is running or has neither an unblocked signal of its own
nor signal work assigned to it by a previous send.
A shared pending signal assigned to another thread does not exclude it.
That thread must not wait for the signal in
.Fn rt_sigtimedwait
.Pq Xr sigaction 2
or a read of a
.Xr signalfd 2 ,
the thread the signal was sent to must not be traced, and the process must
not be an init.
Only
.Dv SIGKILL
needs no more than a thread that does not block it and has not begun to
end.
The parent then dies, as Linux's killable wait lets it.
A signal that is not fatal then waits, pending, until the child calls
.Xr execve 2
or ends.
musl's and GNU libc's
.Fn vfork
make this call.
.Pp
.Fn clone ,
number 120 on i386 and 56 on x86-64, creates a child the way
.Fn fork
does, in the forms GNU libc's
.Fn fork
and both C libraries'
.Fn posix_spawn
use, or a new thread of the caller's process.
Its i386 arguments are, in order, the flags, a stack pointer for the
child, the address of the parent's copy of the child's ID, a TLS
descriptor, and the address of the child's copy.
The low byte of the flags, the signal the parent receives when the child
exits, may be zero or
.Dv SIGCHLD
for a process; a thread sends none.
A zero-signal child's end wakes the parent without posting a signal;
.Xr wait 2
selects it with
.Dv __WCLONE
or
.Dv __WALL .
A successful exec, orphan adoption or a new image of the real parent
restores ordinary SIGCHLD notification.
The flags may also contain
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
which gives the child the parent's address space itself, so a store, a
mapping or a
.Xr brk 2
by either is seen by both, and gives the child no alternate signal stack
unless
.Dv CLONE_VFORK
is set too;
.Dv CLONE_VFORK ,
which makes the parent wait as the parent of
.Fn vfork
waits;
.Dv CLONE_FILES ,
which gives the child the parent's descriptor table itself, so a
descriptor either opens, closes or duplicates is the other's too, and so
are the
.Xr fcntl 2
record locks the table owns, which a close by either drops, until an
.Xr execve 2
succeeds, which gives the caller a copy of its own and leaves the locks
with the table; each of the two is held to its own
.Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE
in the table they share;
.Dv CLONE_FS ,
which gives the child the parent's working directory, root directory and
file-creation mask themselves, so a
.Xr chdir 2 ,
.Xr chroot 2
or
.Xr umask 2
by either changes both, and which fails while a thread that shares them is
inside an
.Xr execve 2
that gives a caller without effective user ID 0 the IDs of a set-ID
program, since that call has chosen them on the ground that no other
process shares its directories;
.Dv CLONE_THREAD
with
.Dv CLONE_SIGHAND ,
which need each other and
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
and which make the child a thread of the caller's process: it has an ID of
its own, which
.Xr gettid 2
returns, and shares the process ID, the parent, the signal dispositions,
the signals sent to the process, and the process's end, as
.Xr exit 2
and
.Xr sigaction 2
describe;
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS ,
which gives the child the TLS base of the descriptor at
.Fa tls ,
as
.Xr set_thread_area 2
takes one;
.Dv CLONE_PARENT ,
which makes the child a child of the caller's parent, as a thread is,
and inherits the caller's exit notification choice;
.Dv CLONE_UNTRACED ,
which prevents a tracer from inheriting the child or receiving its fork
event, without preventing a later explicit attach;
.Dv CLONE_PARENT_SETTID ,
which stores the child's ID at
.Fa parent_tid
in the parent's memory before the child runs;
.Dv CLONE_CHILD_SETTID ,
which stores it at
.Fa child_tid
in the child's memory before the child runs;
.Dv CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID ,
which records
.Fa child_tid
as the address the child's thread end clears, as
.Xr set_thread_area 2
describes for
.Nm set_tid_address ;
.Dv CLONE_PIDFD ,
which stores a pidfd of the child, as an int, at
.Fa parent_tid
in the parent's memory before the child runs, a close-on-exec descriptor
of the parent's that
.Xr pidfd_open 2
describes and the child does not have, a pidfd of the new thread alone with
.Dv CLONE_THREAD ,
as on Linux 6.9 and later, and which takes neither
.Dv CLONE_PARENT_SETTID ,
whose address it uses, nor
.Dv CLONE_DETACHED ;
and
.Dv CLONE_DETACHED
and
.Dv CLONE_SYSVSEM ,
which are ignored, the second as a process's semaphore adjustments are its
own, which its threads share.
A failed store is ignored, as on Linux.
.Dv CLONE_NEWUSER ,
.Dv CLONE_NEWPID ,
.Dv CLONE_NEWNS ,
.Dv CLONE_NEWNET ,
.Dv CLONE_NEWUTS
and
.Dv CLONE_NEWIPC
give the child new namespaces of those kinds, as
.Xr unshare 2
describes; a child made in a new PID namespace is its init, pid 1 there,
and the parent receives its number in the parent's namespace.
A nonzero
.Fa stack
is the stack pointer the child returns to user mode with.
Every other flag is refused.
.Pp
.Fn clone3
takes the same request in Linux's
.Vt struct clone_args ,
which glibc tries before
.Fn clone
for threads and
.Xr posix_spawn 3 :
the flags, with the exit signal in a field of its own, the address
.Dv CLONE_PIDFD
stores the pidfd at, the child and parent thread ID addresses, the lowest
address of the new stack and its size, from whose sum the child starts,
and the TLS descriptor.
Beside
.Fn clone Ns 's
flags it takes
.Dv CLONE_CLEAR_SIGHAND ,
which GNU libc's
.Xr posix_spawn 3
passes with
.Dv CLONE_VM
and
.Dv CLONE_VFORK :
every handler of the child goes back to the default action, a signal the
parent ignores stays ignored, and each has no flags and an empty mask.
Here
.Dv CLONE_PIDFD
may come with
.Dv CLONE_PARENT_SETTID
at another address.
.Fa size
is at least 64, the size of the structure's first version; the fields of
later versions,
.Va set_tid ,
.Va set_tid_size
and
.Va cgroup ,
must be zero.
The caller's registers are unchanged.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 2 for
.Fn fork ,
190 for
.Fn vfork ,
120 for
.Fn clone
and 435 for
.Fn clone3 .
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm fork
is call 57,
.Nm vfork
58,
.Nm clone
56 and
.Nm clone3
435;
.Nm clone
takes the child's tid address before the TLS argument, the order Linux
x86-64 has, where i386 has the TLS argument first, and the TLS argument is
the new thread's FS base.
An i386 call on x86-64 instead supplies a
.Vt user_desc ,
as
.Xr set_thread_area 2
describes, and a selector that names its entry keeps the new descriptor's
base when the child first runs.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The parent receives the child's process ID; the child receives 0.
On failure, the function returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/forktest.c
creates a child that exits with status 42, waits for it, and checks for a
normal exit with that status.
.Pa tests/ofdprobe.c
checks that a child advances the file offset seen by its parent.
.Pa tests/musl/vforkmusl.c
checks that a child made with
.Dv CLONE_VM
and
.Dv CLONE_VFORK
stores into the parent's memory before the parent runs again, that its
.Xr execve 2
leaves the parent's memory whole, that a failed one returns to the child,
and that
.Fn vfork ,
.Fn system
and
.Fn popen
run their children.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOMEM]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn clone
was given a flag other than those above, a process an exit signal other
than zero or
.Dv SIGCHLD ,
.Dv CLONE_THREAD
and
.Dv CLONE_SIGHAND
one without the other or without
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
or
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS
a descriptor
.Xr set_thread_area 2
refuses;
.Dv CLONE_PIDFD
with
.Dv CLONE_DETACHED ,
or with
.Dv CLONE_PARENT_SETTID
at the same address, as
.Fn clone
always has it; or
.Dv CLONE_CLEAR_SIGHAND
with
.Dv CLONE_SIGHAND .
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn clone3
was given a
.Fa size
below 64, a field of a later version that is not zero, a flag past bit
31 other than
.Dv CLONE_CLEAR_SIGHAND ,
.Dv CLONE_INTO_CGROUP
among them, a field other than the flags wider than an address, which is
32 bits on i686, an exit signal in the flags or above 64,
.Dv CLONE_DETACHED ,
or a stack without a size or a size without a stack.
.It Bq Er E2BIG
.Fn clone3
was given a
.Fa size
above 4096, or bytes that are not zero past the structure Linux knows.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS
descriptor or the
.Fn clone3
structure cannot be read, or the
.Dv CLONE_PIDFD
address cannot be written.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
.Dv CLONE_PIDFD
found no free descriptor below the caller's limit.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
Every process ID below
.Pa /proc/sys/kernel/pid_max
is in use, in the first PID namespace or in the child's or one above it;
the number of threads with the parent's real user ID has reached the
parent's soft
.Dv RLIMIT_NPROC
limit; or
.Fn clone
was given
.Dv CLONE_FS
while a thread that shares the caller's directories is inside an
.Xr execve 2
that gives a caller without effective user ID 0 the IDs of a set-ID
program.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel could not allocate the child's process record, page tables,
user page state, kernel stack, memory region records, or descriptor table.
The child's PID namespace, or one above it, has closed allocation after
its init died or its last allocated PID was released by a failed fork.
Failure to copy files or directories, before PID allocation, leaves a
new namespace open; a failure within PID allocation resets its
first-number cursor, as on Linux.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
.Dv CLONE_NEWNS
was given while the mount namespaces of the caller's user, or of the owner of
a user namespace above it, reach the limit in
.Pa /proc/sys/user/max_mnt_namespaces
of that user namespace.
.It Bq Er EPERM
A
.Dv CLONE_NEW
flag other than
.Dv CLONE_NEWUSER
was given by a caller not privileged in its user namespace, or in the new
one the same call makes.
.It Bq Er EPERM
On x86-64,
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS
was given a base that is not below 0x00007ffffffff000, as
.Xr arch_prctl 2
refuses one.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.It Bq Er EUSERS
A user or PID namespace would be nested more than 7 deep.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arch_prctl 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr exit 2 ,
.Xr getpid 2 ,
.Xr pidfd_open 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2 ,
.Xr unshare 2 ,
.Xr waitpid 2
